  "strategy": "**Approach and Rationale**:  \nWe adopt a two-phase strategy to minimize the step function's score. First, we generate a candidate sequence by combining elements of the parent sequence with variations in length and structure (using interpolation on multiple orders). Second, we refine the candidate with a gradient-based optimization, but we limit the length to 512 to ensure feasibility and avoid excessive computation times. This method leverages the parent sequence's existing properties while introducing structural diversity through interpolation and then applying fine-grained optimizations to find a better minimum score.\n\nThe key innovation is maintaining candidate diversity by exploring multiple input lengths (powers of two) and using interpolation to scale the parent sequence into higher dimensions, balancing complexity and optimization effectiveness.\n\n---\n\n**Mathematical Specification**:  \n```\ndef propose_candidate(seed=42):\n    global height_sequence_1\n    n = 512  # Fixed length for search due to computational constraints\n    a = np.zeros(n)  # Placeholder for sequence\n    # Representation: Use a normalized, non-negative candidate of length n.\n    # Objective: argmin_{a \\in \\mathbb{R}^n_+} (2 * n * max_{||convolve(a,a)||} / (sum_{a}^2))\n    # We set constraints: 0 <= a[i] <= 1000 for all i, and sum_{a} >= 0.01 (handled by evaluate_sequence).\n    # Steps: Use stochastic interpolations with mixed odd-even orders to distribute sequence energy near discontinuity to prevent autocorrelation spikes.\n```\n\n---\n\n**Implementation Procedure**:  \n1. **Initial Candidate ($a_0$)**.\n   - If the parent exists:  \n     - $a_0 \\leftarrow \\frac{\\text{interp}(p, \\text{order}=1)}{\\sum}(p)$ where $p$ is the parent sequence, $a_0$ is the interpolated version.\n   - Else:  \n     - Use a uniform sequence of length 256.\n\n2. **Linear Program (LP) Spreading**.\n   - Minimize: $- \\text{score}(candidate)$.\n   - Subject to:  \n     - $candidate \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}_+$  \n     - $\\|convolve(candidate, candidate)\\|_\\infty \\leq M$.\n   - Use first stage of LP refinement to spread the mass.\n\n3. **Gradient Descent Polishing**.\n   - Use Adam optimization on the LP-spread candidate, clipping gradients at a threshold to avoid numerical instability.\n   - Update rule: $x \\leftarrow \\exp(\\text{clip}(x))$ normalized to $\\sum = 0.01$.\n\n4. **Interpolation Diversification**.\n   - Generate new sequences by interpolation of the best candidate:\n     - Pattern: $candidate_{i} \\leftarrow \\text{interp}(best, \\text{order}=i)$ for $i=1,2,3$.\n   - For best candidate not improving, use QS automatically with exponential smoothing.\n\n5. **Fallback**.\n   - If refinement fails, return a Q_perturbed sequence derived from initial Adam perturbation.\n\n---\n\n**Technical Integration**:  \n- Import necessary libraries:\n  ```\n  import numpy as np\n  import scipy.optimize.minimize\n  ```\n- Use pre-evaluated constraints for the convolution (borrowed from standard practice) to avoid arbitrary local dips.\n- Heuristically clip gradients below normalized score thresholds to stabilize iterates.\n\n---\n\n**Compute Budget**:  \n- Allocate up to 150 seconds for Adam polishing, ensuring each evaluation fits into the clock cycle.\n- Set total runs to 10 iterations, with 150 seconds per iteration for Q_re normalizations.\n\n---\n\n**Validation & Return**:  \n- Score: $score = 2 * n * max(convolve(candidate, candidate, \\text{mode}='full')) / (\\sum(candidate))^2$\n- After final validation, return the best candidate sequence.",
